fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1054", description: some(value: "scala-gen emits a hyphenated anthill identifier VERBATIM, producing text that is not Scala -- measured, `def zero-val(): T` is a syntax error, and the spec already says what should happen instead.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-08 by WI-1020's compile harness (commit 0ebec357). `Bootstrap.generate` on stdlib/anthill/prelude/numeric.anthill emits:\n\n  package anthill.prelude\n\n  trait Numeric[T] extends PartialOrd[T]:\n    def add(a: T, b: T): T\n    def sub(a: T, b: T): T\n    def neg(a: T): T\n    def mul(a: T, b: T): T\n    def zero-val(): T\n\n`dotc` 3.8.4 reports `Numeric.scala:8: '=' expected, but identifier found` -- it parses `zero` as the method name, `-` as an operator, and stops. This is the WORST failure class the harness can find: not a type error but a PARSE error, i.e. the emitter produced something that is not the language.\n\nTHE RULE IS ALREADY WRITTEN. docs/scala-forward-mapping.md §1.1 says, in the paragraph describing the output tree: \"Anthill `namespace` segments map directly to the package path; hyphens in identifiers become underscores.\" So this is not an undecided design question -- it is a documented mapping rule with no implementation.\n\nMECHANISM. `Names.scala` handles `_` and nothing else: `scalaTypeName` (:26) and `toCamel` (:35) both branch on `s.contains('_')`, and `scalaFieldName`/the method-name path inherit that. A `-` passes through untouched. Note the repo already knows anthill identifiers contain `-` -- it is why scaland's parser must never re-lex a source slice (WI-950).\n\nDECIDE, and say why: §1.1 says hyphens become UNDERSCORES (`zero_val`), but every other identifier in this backend is camelCased (`to_bigint` -> `toBigint`, pinned by the WI-170 test), so `zeroVal` is the consistent answer and `zero_val` is the documented one. Whichever wins, the OTHER of (code, §1.1) must be updated in the same change -- the current state is that they disagree and neither is enforced.\n\nCHECK THE WHOLE SURFACE, not just operation names: sort names, entity names, field names, and namespace segments can all carry `-`, and each reaches a different `Names` entry point. A hyphen in a namespace segment reaches a PACKAGE name, where the failure mode is different again.\n\nACCEPTANCE: driven by WI-1020's compile harness -- numeric.anthill's emission compiles. A test that a hyphenated name in each position (operation, sort, entity, field) emits a legal Scala identifier, asserting the CHOSEN spelling rather than merely 'not a hyphen'. Say at the test site which assertion fails when the fix is backed out. §1.1 and the code agree at the end of it. sbt test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-1020's prelude sweep, which found 27 of 44 prelude files do not compile."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-10T07:48:41Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1054", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. DECISION: `-` normalizes to `_` FIRST, then §5's per-kind convention runs -- so `zero-val` and `zero_val` reach Scala as ONE name. Net: operation/field `zeroVal`, sort/entity `MySort`, namespace segment `my_lib` (and the `my_lib/` directory). This makes §1.1 and §5 AGREE rather than choosing between them: the underscore §1.1 promises survives in exactly the position §1.1 is written about (the package path), while a kind that IS camelCased takes the camelCase. §1.1/§2.1/§5 all updated; §5 now carries the hyphen rule as its own table.\n\nCODE: `Names.normalize` is one private site; `scalaMethodName`/`scalaTypeName` apply it first and `toCamel` is now PRIVATE so no caller can reach a conversion that skips it. New `Names.scalaPackageSegment` (hyphen rule is its whole conversion -- no camelCase, no backticking) + `Names.scalaPackagePath`. Four package-building sites converted: `namespacePath`, `splitPath`'s multi-segment prefix, `importedNames`, and `ScalaTypes.autoImportPackage`.\n\nCONTROLS MEASURED, one site backed out at a time: scalaMethodName -> corpus + all-positions tests fail; scalaTypeName -> all-positions fails; scalaPackageSegment -> all-positions + import fail; splitPath prefix -> only the dotted-declaration test fails; importedNames alone -> the import test becomes a BootstrapError. Closure ladder RE-MEASURED: 3 -> 2 -> 4 -> clean, down from 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 4 (the parse error removed the first rung outright; the rest unchanged).\n\nLANDMINES for the next reader.\n(1) ONE-SIDED PACKAGE COMPARISON is the trap this ticket kept re-creating. A package string is only ever compared against another package string, so EVERY producer must convert. Missed at first in `importedNames` (a self-import read as foreign -> false Unplaceable) and again in `autoImportPackage` (caught in review: an unconverted table key would empty `autoImportedTypes` silently, degrading every prelude name from arity-checked `Known` to unchecked `Ambient`).\n(2) NORMALIZING WIDENS A MANY-TO-ONE. `scalaTypeName` already mapped `foo_bar`/`fooBar` to one image; `foo-bar` joins that class, so two sorts can now emit one `FooBar.scala`. `emittedTypes`' duplicate check is keyed on the ANTHILL leaf and cannot see it -> added `Bootstrap.refuseColliding` on the OUTPUT (every emission route builds its own relPath), and a `require` in `ScalaCompile.errors`, which silently overwrote duplicate paths and reported a green compile for a tree that could never exist on disk.\n(3) A TEST THAT SCANS THE WHOLE EMITTED FILE FOR `-` IS WRONG. `evidenceNote` legitimately writes raw anthill names into `//` comments, so the scan must skip comment lines or it asserts an invariant the emitter does not hold.\n(4) `NamespacePath.anthillLeaf` is for the DIAGNOSTIC LABEL ALONE -- `scalaTypeName` normalizes for itself and normalization is idempotent, so it is NOT what the `<Ns>Ops` name is read from.\n\nKNOWN GAPS LEFT, each stated at its site rather than fixed: a RESERVED-WORD namespace segment (`namespace type`) still emits verbatim -- backticking would put backticks in a directory name, and refusing needs a span `Names` does not have (§5 records the exception next to the backticking rule); and a namespace's `<Ns>Ops` trait is emitted into the PARENT package, which predates this change and is entangled with nested-namespaces-as-objects being unimplemented.\n\nFOLLOW-UP FILED: WI-1071 -- cpp-gen has the same defect class in all five positions plus the header FILENAME (measured). rust-gen and smt-gen already convert; cpp-gen is the only backend of the three with no identifier conversion at all.\n\nsbt test 431/431 green. /code-review run; its real findings applied (the two one-sided-comparison bugs above, the false whole-file scan, a missing fourth control, a stale closure ledger, and a §5 doc gap).", at: "2026-08-10T07:49:09Z")
